      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dedham was founded in 1635 as one of the earliest inland settlements of the Massachusetts Bay Colony.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Dedham",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early residents wanted to name the town \"Contentment,\" but the General Court chose Dedham after the English town of Dedham, Essex.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dedham%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s seal, a symbol of its heritage, was originally designed by a member of the Dedham Historical Society.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dedham%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mother Brook, the first artificial canal in America, ran through Dedham, powering mills that marked the town’s shift from farming to industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dedham%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Louisa May Alcott, author of Little Women, was born in Dedham, linking the town to a literary icon.",
